WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.594 --> 00:00:04.760
PROFESSOR: I love ice cream.

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:08.840
It's one of life's simple pleasures, but
the science that's much more complex.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.660
At its core, ice cream is a
product of phase transition,

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:14.120
in this case, water freezing into ice.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.460
Two things that greatly impact
the texture of ice cream

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.760
are the amount of the water and
ice cream base that freezes,

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.477
and how quickly that
transition takes place.

00:00:22.477 --> 00:00:24.560
So one of the biggest
challenges for the home cook

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:29.360
is making ice cream that is dense,
smooth, and free of noticeable iciness.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.460
What's interesting is that
all things being equal,

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.310
ice cream that we perceive as
icy, doesn't actually contain

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:36.580
more ice crystals, just bigger ones.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:39.920
The crystals in smooth ice cream are
so small that our tongues can't even

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.460
detect them.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:45.350
So there are two basic ways to prevent
ice cream from having an icy texture.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:48.440
The first is to control
the water in the base.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:51.360
Now this could mean reducing
the overall water content.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.900
It could also mean keeping more
of the water in liquid form

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.490
by adjusting sweeteners.

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:58.190
Sweeteners lower the freezing
point of the water in the mixture,

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:00.770
preventing some of it
from turning to ice.

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:04.400
Or you can use ingredients like
starches and gums, which trap free water

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.560
and prevent it from
forming large ice crystals.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:08.690
The second way to
prevent an icy texture is

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:12.629
to make sure that the ice crystals
that do form are as small as possible.

00:01:12.629 --> 00:01:14.420
Here, we're talking
about rate of freezing.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:18.350
The faster you freeze ice cream, the
smaller the ice crystals will be.

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:20.170
Now commercial ice
cream manufacturers have

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:23.470
a lot of advantages over the home cook,
when it comes to making ice cream.

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:26.080
They have powerful stabilizers
and professional freezers

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:29.440
and blast chillers that can turn a
liquid custard into a semi-solid ice

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.127
cream in a matter of seconds.

00:01:31.127 --> 00:01:33.460
It's not something you could
do at home, and believe me,

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.711
in America's Test Kitchen, we tried to.

00:01:35.711 --> 00:01:37.960
So here are the ingredients
for our vanilla ice cream.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.210
I'm using a combination
of heavy cream and milk

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:41.840
for the right level of richness.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.590
Now fat also helps reduce the
perception of ice crystals

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:45.990
by lubricating your tongue.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.880
Now I'm using fresh vanilla
bean for its complex flavor,

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:52.330
and I'm going to add it early in the
process the maximum flavor out of it.

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:55.814
So next up, we have the sweeteners,
which have a huge impact on texture.

00:01:55.814 --> 00:01:57.730
I'll come back to that
point in just a minute.

00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:00.830
Here, I'm using granulated
sugar, which is pretty common,

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:02.980
and corn syrup, which is not.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:06.870
Corn syrup is a combination of sweet
glucose molecules, which make it sweet,

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.830
and chains of broken cornstarch.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:11.530
So it's these chains
of tangled cornstarch

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.180
which give corn syrup it's viscosity.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:15.610
Now, in our ice cream, they
prevent water molecules

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:18.010
from grouping and freezing
into big crystals.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:20.760
PROFESSOR: So since we're thinking
about ice cream, at the moment,

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:22.210
let's imagine freezing it.

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:25.250
If we cool it, then we know
that without the sugar or salt,

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:29.830
the temperature at which
the water becomes a solid

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:30.970
is zero degrees Celsius.

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:34.150
And that's because the entropy of
the water molecules moving around

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:37.570
becomes balanced by the sticking energy
of that water molecules to each other

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:39.400
to make the crystal.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.460
If there's salt or sugar in
the water, that salt and sugar

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:45.730
has an additional entropy that is
besides the entropy of the water

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:46.280
molecules.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:48.154
There's also an entropy
of the salt molecule.

00:02:48.154 --> 00:02:51.280
So you can imagine these little
molecules jiggling around.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:52.990
And when you freeze
the mixture, you have

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:58.160
to also stop the motion of the solute
molecules of the sugar or salt.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:01.090
And that requires a little
extra energy than you

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:03.530
would have had to give it otherwise.

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:06.160
So this extra energy
leads to the phenomenon

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:09.080
that people call freezing
point depression.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:11.440
This is the notion that
if you actually try

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:14.840
to freeze a mixture of
salt water or sugar water,

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.930
you have to actually go
to a lower temperature

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:19.930
to freeze it than you would if
there were no salt and sugar.

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:23.350
That lower temperature
is to actually get rid

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:28.330
of the extra energy that's needed to
actually stop the solute from moving.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:31.930
Freezing point depression essentially
depends on the entropy of the solute.

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:35.260
And as such, the amount that you have
to actually depress the freezing point

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:39.070
really depends on the number of
solute molecules per unit volume

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:40.510
that you've managed to put in.

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:44.440
The more you put in, the more
the freezing point is depressed.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:48.350
If we think about this when making ice
cream, it raises a bit of a conundrum.

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:50.650
So if you make ice
cream, you all recall,

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:53.110
or you will know soon
enough, when you do the lab,

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:55.120
that the recipe for
ice cream, essentially,

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:59.082
involves taking milk, which of
course has little fat globules

00:03:59.082 --> 00:04:00.040
and other things in it.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:03.460
It's basically water with fat
globules and other molecules.

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:06.760
And you then poor sugar in the milk,
because you'd like it to be sweet.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.030
And then you need to
freeze the sweet milk.

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:14.080
Now, if you freeze the sweet milk by
surrounding it by just normal ice, just

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:16.390
normal frozen water, it won't freeze.

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:19.570
And the reason is is because a
freezing point depression the mixture

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:21.950
has a lower freezing
point than the water.

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:27.370
So somehow, you have to get the material
that's around the milk with sugar in it

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:30.610
to have a lower freezing
point than that of the milk,

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:34.210
and a way to do that is to
add salt. So if you add salt,

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:37.450
you will, of course, depress the
freezing point of the salt water.

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:40.930
If you then freeze that, it
will have a lower temperature

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:43.990
than the freezing point
of the milk-sugar mixture,

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:46.644
and that will allow you to
freeze it and make ice cream.

00:04:46.644 --> 00:04:49.060
PROFESSOR: So let's scoop some
and see what it looks like.

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:57.630
So as you can see, it's
nice and thick and dense.

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:03.230
And I'll take a little
try, just to make sure it's

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:05.330
good and doesn't have any ice.

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:11.300
That's good.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:14.720
It's really nice and dense, very smooth,
and there's really no noticeable ice

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:16.790
crystals, because we
made them nice and small.

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:20.506
So by understanding and controlling
the phase transition of water to ice,

00:05:20.506 --> 00:05:22.880
we get ice cream that's
scoopable right from the freezer.

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:27.430
It's dense, it's creamy, and most
importantly, perfectly smooth.

